.TH fgrep 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
fgrep \- print lines containing a fixed string
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fgrep
[\fB\-chlnsv\fR] [\fB\-e\fR \fIstring\fR] ... [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] ... [\fIstring\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fgrep
searches each
.I file
for lines containing any of the given strings, and prints those that do.
The strings are taken literally: no character means anything but itself,
so nothing needs quoting against the shell except what the shell itself
would eat.
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
it reads standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Print only a count of matching lines per file.
.TP
.BI \-e " string"
Add
.I string
to the set searched for.  May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the strings to search for from
.IR file ,
one per line.  May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-h
Do not prefix output lines with the file name, even with several files.
.TP
.B \-l
Print only the names of files that contain a match.
.TP
.B \-n
Prefix each line with its line number.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress error messages about files that cannot be read.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the lines that do
.I not
match.
.PP
If neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
is given, the first non-option argument is the string.
.SH NOTES
There is no
.BR \-i :
.B fgrep
is case sensitive and cannot be told otherwise.  Use
.BR "grep \-i" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if any line matched, 1 if none did.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR awk (1)
